<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Venetian Monemvasia</h1><p class="st-meta">1464 CE – 1540 CE · capital Monemvasia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Venetian rule over the rock fortress of Monemvasia, the last free Byzantine town in the Peloponnese and home of malmsey wine.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a>, a fortified town on a rock island off the Laconian coast, belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> in 1460, the despot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-palaiologos">Thomas Palaiologos</a> handed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a>. Papal rule proved weak, and in 1464 the inhabitants placed themselves under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Venetian officials governed with the town's Greek notables, maintaining its fortifications and port. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a>, called Malvasia by the Italians, gave its name to malmsey, a sweet wine traded across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, although by this time most of it came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. The town was largely cut off from its Ottoman-held hinterland.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the peace of 1540 that ended the third Ottoman-Venetian war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. It returned to Venetian control from 1690 to 1715 as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/venetian-morea">Venetian Morea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1464 CE – 1540 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.84%;width:1.51%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1464 CE – 1540 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Monemvasia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Venetian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malvasia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1502&amp;focus=venetian-monemvasia" aria-label="Venetian Monemvasia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 855 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Venetian Monemvasia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5579 1111L5560 1098M5579 1111L5293 831M5579 1111L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5560 1098h0M5293 831h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5579 1111h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5579 1111h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Monemvasia. <a href="/atlas?year=1502&amp;focus=venetian-monemvasia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
